{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head 6f292a1eb02c3efa41039d768dbcb8fdcdc26824, addressing all four findings in 8393d8d1949dc0bdbfc8d73f84f6b5a05509e2ba. Gates green: vet, test, diff-check, clean worktree.\n\nThe three blockers share one cause, and it is the same one you have now named three rounds running: I describe a mechanism and leave its objects implicit. F1 is the clearest instance. Step 1 renamed each inbox ref to a claimed ref, which leaves it pointing at the submitter-authored candidate commit — a commit that cannot record a drain run's parent or batch — and every later paragraph then reasoned about a claim commit that step 1 never created. Your second point about per-candidate claims is the one I had not seen at all: an N-candidate drain leaves N claims against one published batch head, so the early claims observe a head later than their own and are classified as anomalies by the table meant to protect them. It is now one batch claim per run, created atomically with all corresponding inbox deletions, keeping every candidate and every prebuilt sequence commit reachable so nothing depends on a ref that the same push is deleting.\n\nF2 I verified rather than accepted: gitstore.SignedCommit sets GIT_AUTHOR_DATE and GIT_COMMITTER_DATE from time.Now(), so replaying identical inputs a second later yields a different commit id, and \"the expected head is computable from the claim\" was a promise the implementation cannot keep. The run now prebuilds the sequence commits before claiming — which is what makes the exact head E knowable at all rather than an optimisation — and records E, P and M in the claim.\n\nF3: the omitted state is the commonest interruption, which is worse than an exotic gap. A crash after the mirror advance but before release leaves published=E, mirror=E and a live claim, matching no case, so an ordinary crash fell off the end of the classification. The table now carries four live-claim rows plus the quiescent one, a start invariant of no new claim unless published=mirror=P, and an explicit no-claim rule: if the two refs diverge with no claim to explain it, refuse and ask for manual reconciliation rather than infer which is right. That is the lost-claim case, which is why claim loss is now deliberately tested and why claims sit behind a ruleset that stops a submitter deleting them. Multiple outstanding claims are excluded structurally by the single batch claim rather than enumerated.\n\nF4: steps 3 and 4 and spike case 6 were describing two different protocols, one releasing after the sequence push and the other after mirror convergence. They are one state machine now, and case 8 asserts mirror convergence before release at each of the three crash points, so it can no longer pass while exercising the behaviour that was replaced. Case 6 must exercise every row of the table with N candidates rather than a representative sample with one, and every tuple the table does not name must refuse without moving either durable ref.\n\nThank you for confirming the intent-text anchor, the encoder/candidate split and the three-run coalescing case; those are settled and I have not touched them.","body":{"commit":"6f292a1eb02c3efa41039d768dbcb8fdcdc26824","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}